{"id":"h113","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"Does an ideal gas get hotter or colder when it expands according to the law pV^2 = Const?\nIs the correct answer \"colder\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"For an ideal gas, temperature is proportional to pV.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Along pV^2 = const, pV = C/V, so T is proportional to 1/V.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Temperature falls as volume increases, so the expanding gas gets colder.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The result holds whatever the gas's heat capacity, since it follows only from the equation of state and process law.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Positive work done in expansion suggests internal energy loss and cooling, but this is not the deciding argument.","confidence":0.5,"lean":1,"theta":0.7854,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Pressure falls faster (p ∝ V^-2) than temperature, so the temperature drop is more gradual than the pressure drop.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Heat exchange could push temperature the other way, but this is dismissed because the process law already encodes the heat flow.","confidence":0.2,"lean":-1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The ideal gas law combined with the process law decides the answer.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":-0.5,"relation":"cont